<DocsLayout title="Offhook API" lead="One REST API to place and answer AI phone calls, using your own speech-to-text, text-to-speech, language model and phone carrier." slug="">
        <p>
          Offhook runs the parts of a voice agent that are tedious to build yourself:
          streaming audio between the caller and the models, dialing reliably, keeping a
          complete log of every call, and sending signed webhooks. You bring the provider
          keys and describe the agent.
        </p>

        <h2 id="base-url">Base URL</h2>
        <p>You send requests to your own deployment. In local development that is:</p>
        <Code tabs={[{ label: "Base URL", lang: "text", code: "http://localhost:8000" }]} />
        <Callout kind="note">
          Every endpoint in these docs is relative to that address. For example,
          <code>POST /v1/calls</code> means <code>POST http://localhost:8000/v1/calls</code>.
        </Callout>

        <h2 id="model">How the pieces fit</h2>
        <p>There are three resources, and each one builds on the one before it:</p>
        <div class="chips">
          <span class="chip"><b>1</b> Connections: your provider keys</span>
          <span class="chip">→</span>
          <span class="chip"><b>2</b> Agents: who talks, and with which connections</span>
          <span class="chip">→</span>
          <span class="chip"><b>3</b> Calls: an agent dialing a number</span>
        </div>
        <ul>
          <li><a href="/docs/connections/">Connections</a> store your provider credentials (Deepgram, Cartesia, Groq, Plivo and others), encrypted.</li>
          <li><a href="/docs/agents/">Agents</a> combine instructions, a voice and the connections to run on.</li>
          <li><a href="/docs/calls/">Calls</a> use an agent to place an outbound call. <a href="/docs/inbound/">Inbound calls</a> use one to answer.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2 id="auth">Authentication</h2>
        <p>
          Every request carries an API key as a bearer token. Keys start with
          <code>vk_dev_</code> or <code>vk_prod_</code>, depending on the environment that
          created them. See <a href="/docs/authentication/">Authentication</a>.
        </p>
        <Code tabs={[{ label: "cURL", lang: "bash", code: `curl http://localhost:8000/v1/calls \\\n  -H "Authorization: Bearer $OFFHOOK_KEY"` }]} />

        <h2 id="next">Next steps</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><a href="/docs/quickstart/">Quickstart</a>: run Offhook locally and place your first call.</li>
          <li><a href="/docs/webhooks/">Webhooks</a>: get call events as they happen.</li>
          <li><a href="/docs/errors/">Errors and limits</a>: status codes, call error codes and rate limits.</li>
        </ul>

</DocsLayout>
